1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.809
[S01] So we're gonna start that today by talking about something we've really ignored

2
00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:09.380
[S01] in the course so far, which is uncertainty.

3
00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:14.939
[S01] And how uncertainty affects your decision making.

4
00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:19.640
[S01] So we've done so far in this class is we've sort of said, look, we've assumed whatever

5
00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.739
[S01] you make decisions, you make them with full knowledge and full certainty.

6
00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:28.989
[S01] But many, many decisions in life are made under conditions of uncertainty.

7
00:00:29.019 --> 00:00:33.679
[S01] Okay, so consider your decision to study for the final in this class.

8
00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:39.020
[S01] In our model so far, you could optimize your studying across different units of the class.

9
00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.979
[S01] So our model so far, you'd know on the final which unit would be represented with what

10
00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:45.960
[S01] proportion, and you'd optimize your studying appropriately.

11
00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.780
[S01] But of course, you don't know that.

12
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.259
[S01] You face uncertainty about what will be covered in the final and what proportion.

13
00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:54.060
[S01] You know, the whole course is, you're responsible for the whole class.

14
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.780
[S01] But obviously allocate your time, you're uncertain about how to allocate your time

15
00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.979
[S01] across the different subjects on the test.

16
00:01:00.009 --> 00:01:02.219
[S01] So how do you make that decision?

17
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:07.840
[S01] Nothing, we need to bring our tools to bear on thinking about these kinds of decision

18
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:11.200
[S01] making under uncertainty situations.

19
00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:15.730
[S01] And this isn't just about the test, you know, I decide whether to bring my umbrella today.

20
00:01:15.769 --> 00:01:18.060
[S01] Okay, if I bring my umbrella, there's a chance I'm gonna lose it, but I don't wanna get

21
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.609
[S01] wet, I have to think about whether it's gonna rain, it all depends on how certain I am it's

22
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.099
[S01] gonna rain, etc.

23
00:01:23.129 --> 00:01:28.280
[S01] There's decisions about, you know, whether to bet on a sporting event.

24
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.290
[S01] That's decision uncertainty, and that's all the fun stuff in your life.

25
00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:33.750
[S01] We get to the anxiety ridden adult life.

26
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.209
[S01] You've got things like whether to buy a seven year mortgage or a 30 year mortgage, whether

27
00:01:38.239 --> 00:01:41.609
[S01] to buy health insurance, what school to put your kids in.

28
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.060
[S01] All these things involve a huge amount of uncertainty.

29
00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:48.810
[S01] And we have not yet developed the tools to deal with this.

30
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:56.489
[S01] What's really cool is that economics has a very useful tool to think about exactly

31
00:01:56.519 --> 00:02:00.569
[S01] these kinds of situations, that much like the other tools who dealt this semester,

32
00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:05.170
[S01] is pretty easy once you understand it, but it's a huge amount of power for explaining

33
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.170
[S01] the world.

34
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:14.659
[S01] And that's the tool of expected utility theory.

35
00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:17.780
[S01] And that's what we'll focus on today, that's what we'll learn today, the tool of expected

36
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.469
[S01] utility theory.

37
00:02:19.509 --> 00:02:23.310
[S01] Okay, now, I'm gonna ask you a question, as always, I don't want you to try to outsmart

38
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.780
[S01] me, I just want a quick gut reaction.

39
00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:29.610
[S01] Okay, I'm gonna offer you a bet, not really, but imagine I was.

40
00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:36.250
[S01] I'm gonna flip a coin, heads you get 125 dollars, tails you give me 100 dollars.

41
00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:41.879
[S01] So you win 125 versus lose 100, how many of you take that bet?

42
00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:46.060
[S01] Okay, so, yeah, you're a slightly more aggressive class than usual, about 40% of

43
00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:49.120
[S01] you taking the bet, usually I get more, about 20, 25%.

44
00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.689
[S01] Okay, now, yeah?

45
00:02:52.719 --> 00:02:55.289
[S01] Uh, no, it's a one time bet.

46
00:02:55.319 --> 00:03:00.530
[S01] Okay, so basically, now, how do we think about this, whether it's a good idea or not?

47
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.219
[S01] Now, those who raised your hand probably quickly did the math and did in your head

48
00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:09.509
[S01] what we call an expected value, expected value calculation.

49
00:03:09.539 --> 00:03:11.900
[S01] What's the expected value of any gamble?

50
00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:20.719
[S01] The expected value of any gamble is the probability that you win times what you win, plus the

51
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.340
[S01] probability that you lose times what you lose.

52
00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:28.719
[S01] So you did that calculation in your head, you said, as long as he's not cheating using

53
00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:38.939
[S01] a fair coin, there's a 0.5 times 125, plus 0.5 times minus 100.

54
00:03:38.969 --> 00:03:46.810
[S01] Okay, and that is an expected value of 1250.

55
00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.120
[S01] So those who raised your hand probably did that calculation quickly in your head and

56
00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:53.169
[S01] said, yeah, this is a positive expected value.

57
00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:59.719
[S01] This is what we call in economics, a more than fair bet.

58
00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:00.939
[S01] More than fair.

59
00:04:00.969 --> 00:04:05.289
[S01] A fair bet is one with an expected value of zero.

60
00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:08.250
[S01] So a fair bet is one that the expected value is zero.

61
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.310
[S01] So a bet with a positive expected value is more than fair.

62
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:17.250
[S01] And you in your head said, I did that through, it's more than fair.

63
00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:21.120
[S01] And that's why some of you raised your hands, but many of you didn't.

64
00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:23.019
[S01] And that doesn't mean you were wrong.

65
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.199
[S01] It just means this is not the right way to think about it.

66
00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.769
[S01] Okay, the right way to think about it is that what you care about is not dollars.

67
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.180
[S01] What you care about is utility.

68
00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:34.910
[S01] Dollars are meaningless.

69
00:04:34.939 --> 00:04:38.139
[S01] What you care about as a consumer is your utility.

70
00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:41.889
[S01] And so we don't wanna think about expected value.

71
00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:45.139
[S01] We wanna think about expected utility.

72
00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:46.680
[S01] Now, what is expected utility?

73
00:04:46.699 --> 00:04:50.769
[S01] It's the same kind of formula as this, but with one change.

74
00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:57.389
[S01] Expected utility is the probability that you win times your utility if you win.

75
00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:01.610
[S02] Plus the probability that you lose

76
00:05:03.319 --> 00:05:05.209
[S02] times your utility if you lose.

77
00:05:07.819 --> 00:05:10.310
[S02] And that is somewhat different than expected value.

78
00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:16.370
[S02] And the reason is because utility is not a linear weighting of dollars.

79
00:05:17.089 --> 00:05:19.519
[S02] Utility is a concave weighting of dollars.

80
00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.800
[S02] Okay, as such, because utility is a concave weighting of dollars,

81
00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:29.509
[S02] it exhibits diminishing marginal rate of, uh, diminishing marginal utility,

82
00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:31.279
[S02] diminishing marginal rate of substitution.

83
00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:36.620
[S02] Diminishing marginal rate of substitution, as we talked about ad nauseam in consumer theory.

84
00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:38.329
[S02] Okay.

85
00:05:39.529 --> 00:05:45.379
[S02] That means that the next dollar is worth less to you than the previous dollar.

86
00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:50.180
[S02] Okay, the next dollar is worth, just think of diminishing utility.

87
00:05:50.329 --> 00:05:52.939
[S02] The next slice of pizza is worth less to you than the previous slice of pizza.

88
00:05:53.209 --> 00:05:56.420
[S02] Likewise, the next dollar is worth less to you than the previous dollar.

89
00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:03.410
[S02] As a result, losing a dollar makes you sadder than winning a dollar makes you happy.

90
00:06:04.069 --> 00:06:08.209
[S02] There's a non-linearity that comes from diminishing marginal utility,

91
00:06:08.209 --> 00:06:09.800
[S02] diminishing marginal rate of substitution.

92
00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:13.910
[S02] So for example, let's think about our typical utility function

93
00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:15.709
[S02] that we used previously to consumer theory.

94
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.319
[S02] Utility equals the square root of consumption.

95
00:06:18.889 --> 00:06:21.290
[S02] And let's just say you consume all your income, as we always did.

96
00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:23.389
[S02] We've talked about savings the last couple lectures.

97
00:06:23.509 --> 00:06:26.660
[S02] Let's put savings aside again and just assume people consume all their income.

98
00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:29.899
[S02] Okay, and so utility is the square root of consumption.

99
00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:36.649
[S02] Okay, and let's say you start, your initial consumption, C zero, is a hundred.

100
00:06:37.069 --> 00:06:39.560
[S02] You start with a hundred dollars of consumption.

101
00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:43.069
[S02] Okay, okay, your C zero is a hundred.

102
00:06:43.459 --> 00:06:47.329
[S02] So your initial utility, U zero, is 10.

103
00:06:49.639 --> 00:06:52.850
[S02] Now, I give you, I offer you this bet.

104
00:06:53.329 --> 00:06:55.279
[S02] What's the expected utility of this bet?

105
00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:59.420
[S02] Well, the expected utility of this bet is the probability you win,

106
00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.480
[S02] 0.5 times utility if you win.

107
00:07:03.259 --> 00:07:06.949
[S02] Well, utility if you win is the square root of your consumption if you win.

108
00:07:07.519 --> 00:07:09.139
[S02] What is your consumption if you win?

109
00:07:11.449 --> 00:07:13.209
[S02] If you win the bet, what is your consumption?

110
00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:15.620
[S02] So raise your hand and tell me.

111
00:07:17.269 --> 00:07:20.209
[S02] Starting with a hundred, and I made this bet with you, yeah?

112
00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:20.899
[S03] Two twenty-five.

113
00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:21.740
[S02] Two twenty-five.

114
00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:24.459
[S02] You started with a hundred and you won one twenty-five.

115
00:07:25.149 --> 00:07:26.600
[S02] If you lose the bet,

116
00:07:28.209 --> 00:07:28.959
[S02] what do you have?

117
00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:34.339
[S02] What do you have if you lose the bet?

118
00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.370
[S02] Zero, okay?

119
00:07:38.029 --> 00:07:43.370
[S02] So your expected utility is 0.5 times this utility of what you get if you win,

120
00:07:44.509 --> 00:07:47.360
[S02] plus 0.5 times utility what if you lose.

121
00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:49.250
[S02] Now, if you do that math,

122
00:07:50.029 --> 00:07:53.930
[S02] you will find that that is 7.5.

123
00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:58.069
[S02] Your expected utility of this bet is 7.5,

124
00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:01.819
[S02] which is less than your initial utility.

125
00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.160
[S02] So you should not take the bet,

126
00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:09.110
[S02] which is through the mechanism of psychology, why many of you didn't.

127
00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:12.410
[S02] Okay, you should not take the bet.

128
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:19.220
[S02] Okay, and the reason is because you are what we call risk,

129
00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:22.339
[S02] and humans are what we call risk averse.

130
00:08:24.709 --> 00:08:30.050
[S02] That we, that risk is inherently negative value to us.

131
00:08:30.379 --> 00:08:35.059
[S02] That a do, a certain dollar is worth much more to us than an uncertain dollar.

132
00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.210
[S02] Just like a dollar today is worth more than a dollar tomorrow.

133
00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:41.360
[S02] A certain dollar is worth much more than an uncertain dollar.

134
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:45.830
[S02] To see why, the best way to do this intuition here is graphical.

135
00:08:46.159 --> 00:08:47.840
[S02] So let's go to figure 20 dash 1,

136
00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.090
[S02] and just sort of slowly walk through this.

137
00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:51.470
[S02] It's a little confusing graph.

138
00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:55.639
[S02] On the x-axis is your wealth, your total consumption or money you have.

139
00:08:55.669 --> 00:08:56.809
[S02] You consume everything you have.

140
00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:58.820
[S02] So it's wealth or alternatively consumption.

141
00:08:59.929 --> 00:09:02.750
[S02] On the y-axis of figure 20 dash 1 is your utility.

142
00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.740
[S02] It's a graph of how much you consume against your utility.

143
00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:10.789
[S02] And as you can see, this is a concave graph.

144
00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:14.360
[S02] It is, it's exhibiting diminishing marginal utility.

145
00:09:15.129 --> 00:09:19.570
[S02] Okay, each dollar, each dollar of wealth adds utility,

146
00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.379
[S02] but less and less over time.

147
00:09:21.799 --> 00:09:25.490
[S02] Just like each slice of pizza makes you happier, but less and less over time.

148
00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.009
[S02] Okay, you get that, so the shape of this curve

149
00:09:30.759 --> 00:09:35.120
[S02] is true for any utility function that features diminishing marginal utility.

150
00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:37.399
[S02] That's pretty much any utility function we have used this semester,

151
00:09:37.789 --> 00:09:38.899
[S02] except we're trying to trick you.

152
00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.539
[S02] Okay, has diminishing marginal utility.

153
00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:44.059
[S02] So as a result, it has this shape.

154
00:09:45.259 --> 00:09:48.759
[S02] Okay, now, with the utility function of this shape,

155
00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:52.879
[S02] let's evaluate the gamble I just gave you.

156
00:09:54.129 --> 00:09:57.679
[S02] You start with wealth of a hundred.

157
00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:00.129
[S02] So you see in the x-axis, the hundred point.

158
00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:05.210
[S04] That corresponds to utility of 10, right?

159
00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:08.840
[S04] So you can trace up that 100 to utility curve,

160
00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:11.149
[S04] and then you go over to the y-axis.

161
00:10:11.179 --> 00:10:13.820
[S04] So that corresponds to utility of 10, okay?

162
00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:17.539
[S04] Now, think about what the gamble does.

163
00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:19.370
[S04] What the gamble does is say, look,

164
00:10:19.399 --> 00:10:22.429
[S04] there's two possible outcomes with 50% chance each.

165
00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:25.370
[S04] One is wealth of 225.

166
00:10:25.399 --> 00:10:27.830
[S04] That is the point all the way to the right.

167
00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:30.799
[S04] That leaves utility of 15.

168
00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:32.929
[S04] The other is wealth of 0.

169
00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:35.240
[S04] That's the point all the way to the left.

170
00:10:35.269 --> 00:10:38.330
[S04] That leaves utility of 0.

171
00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:39.860
[S04] What is the average of those two?

172
00:10:39.889 --> 00:10:42.230
[S04] Well, it's just a linear combination, right?

173
00:10:42.259 --> 00:10:44.029
[S04] It's a 50% chance of each.

174
00:10:44.059 --> 00:10:46.669
[S04] So the average of those two

175
00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:50.059
[S04] is a wealth of 112.5,

176
00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:53.120
[S04] but an expected utility of 7.5.

177
00:10:53.149 --> 00:10:56.539
[S04] So you draw a chord between those two points,

178
00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:58.879
[S04] between the 0 point and point B.

179
00:10:58.909 --> 00:11:01.070
[S04] You find the midpoint of that chord.

180
00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:02.840
[S04] That's wealth of 112.5.

181
00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:06.230
[S04] But then you trace that over to utility function.

182
00:11:06.259 --> 00:11:11.840
[S04] And you see the expected utility is only 7.5.

183
00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:15.919
[S04] And that's because we're not using a linear combination.

184
00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:17.929
[S04] We're using a nonlinear combination,

185
00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.179
[S04] a nonlinear concave combination,

186
00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:23.240
[S04] which means that moving up in terms of wealth

187
00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:24.769
[S04] makes you less happy

188
00:11:24.799 --> 00:11:26.990
[S04] than moving down in terms of wealth makes you sad.

189
00:11:27.019 --> 00:11:29.600
[S04] And you're really sad at 0.

190
00:11:29.629 --> 00:11:33.529
[S04] So it's going from 100 down to 0 makes you way sadder.

191
00:11:33.559 --> 00:11:37.129
[S04] The go from 100 up to 225 makes you happier.

192
00:11:37.159 --> 00:11:40.669
[S04] And that's all because diminishing marginal utility of income.

193
00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:44.750
[S04] So it's natural that this gamble would make you worse off,

194
00:11:44.779 --> 00:11:47.480
[S04] even though it's more than fair.

195
00:11:47.509 --> 00:11:49.549
[S04] Because yeah, you're somewhat happier if you win.

196
00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:52.789
[S04] If you win, utility goes up from 10 to 15.

197
00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.259
[S04] That's great.

198
00:11:54.289 --> 00:11:56.629
[S04] But if you lose, utility goes down from 10 to 0.

199
00:11:56.659 --> 00:11:58.370
[S04] That's really bad.

200
00:11:58.399 --> 00:12:01.789
[S04] So you don't want to take this risk, okay?

201
00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:04.070
[S04] Which is why, although you may not realize it,

202
00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:08.149
[S04] many of you wouldn't want to take that gamble.

203
00:12:08.179 --> 00:12:10.159
[S04] So using this graph, we can ask, okay,

204
00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:12.049
[S04] there are questions about that?

205
00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:12.620
[S04] Yeah?

206
00:12:12.649 --> 00:12:16.659
[S05] Do we take the bet if our unitarity outweighs our loss's utility?

207
00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:20.379
[S05] Or will we not take the bet if our utility is lower?

208
00:12:20.409 --> 00:12:23.960
[S04] Well, you've answered, it's the same.

209
00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:25.370
[S04] The answers to those two questions are the same.

210
00:12:25.399 --> 00:12:26.779
[S04] It just depends on the probabilities.

211
00:12:26.809 --> 00:12:28.940
[S04] If the probabilities are 0.5 each,

212
00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:31.460
[S04] then those two answers, two questions are exactly the same.

213
00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:34.279
[S04] Because 0.5 each would be gain outweighing,

214
00:12:34.309 --> 00:12:36.379
[S04] loss is the same as on net positive.

215
00:12:36.409 --> 00:12:37.639
[S04] But if the probabilities aren't 0.5,

216
00:12:37.669 --> 00:12:39.409
[S04] you want to use this equation.

