642
00:39:56.289 --> 00:40:00.179
[S16] How do we know that theory is wrong?

643
00:40:00.210 --> 00:40:05.409
[S16] How do we know that theory is on its face wrong, that people are risk-loving?

644
00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:05.909
[S16] Yeah?

645
00:40:09.710 --> 00:40:11.849
[S18] Well, that's one way we might know.

646
00:40:11.849 --> 00:40:13.610
[S18] But how do we know more globally?

647
00:40:13.610 --> 00:40:15.469
[S18] You guys could be a weird bunch.

648
00:40:15.469 --> 00:40:16.349
[S18] How do we know more globally?

649
00:40:16.349 --> 00:40:16.849
[S18] Yeah?

650
00:40:18.989 --> 00:40:22.349
[S18] Yeah, but that's absolutely right theoretically.

651
00:40:22.349 --> 00:40:24.050
[S18] But theoretically, in the real world,

652
00:40:24.050 --> 00:40:25.550
[S18] what piece of evidence can you immediately point to

653
00:40:25.550 --> 00:40:28.119
[S18] that I recently pointed out that could show this is wrong?

654
00:40:28.119 --> 00:40:28.619
[S18] Yeah?

655
00:40:29.349 --> 00:40:30.690
[S18] People buy insurance.

656
00:40:30.690 --> 00:40:32.969
[S18] If they're risk-loving, why they buy an insurance?

657
00:40:32.969 --> 00:40:34.369
[S18] So clearly, people aren't risk-loving.

658
00:40:34.369 --> 00:40:36.610
[S18] They wouldn't spend 10% of GDP on insurance.

659
00:40:36.610 --> 00:40:37.769
[S18] So that theory is clearly wrong.

660
00:40:38.969 --> 00:40:39.710
[S18] Okay?

661
00:40:39.710 --> 00:40:40.570
[S18] That's theory one.

662
00:40:41.449 --> 00:40:44.590
[S18] Now, the second theory is a somewhat subtler version

663
00:40:44.590 --> 00:40:46.199
[S18] of this theory, which is quite interesting.

664
00:40:47.369 --> 00:40:51.269
[S18] Which is that people are both risk averse and risk-loving.

665
00:40:52.309 --> 00:40:54.650
[S18] So that risk aversion varies.

666
00:40:55.250 --> 00:40:59.489
[S18] Risk tolerance, let's call it, varies.

667
00:41:00.170 --> 00:41:02.869
[S18] And then in particular, people are risk averse

668
00:41:02.869 --> 00:41:06.409
[S18] over small gambles, but risk-loving over big gambles.

669
00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:09.369
[S18] So let's look at figure 20-4.

670
00:41:10.289 --> 00:41:13.010
[S18] This is an example of what we call Friedman-Savage preferences.

671
00:41:13.010 --> 00:41:14.210
[S18] You don't need to know that.

672
00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:18.179
[S18] But they had this idea that maybe people are locally risk averse,

673
00:41:18.179 --> 00:41:19.610
[S18] but globally risk-loving.

674
00:41:20.179 --> 00:41:20.960
[S18] Let's see what this...

675
00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:21.460
[S18] Let's give it...

676
00:41:21.460 --> 00:41:22.889
[S18] Let me explain this, okay?

677
00:41:23.679 --> 00:41:24.820
[S18] This is sort of complicated.

678
00:41:25.369 --> 00:41:30.170
[S18] So imagine that I'm going to offer you a 50-50 gamble

679
00:41:31.969 --> 00:41:35.309
[S18] between W1 and W3.

680
00:41:36.289 --> 00:41:39.250
[S18] So a gamble leads you to W1, a gamble leads to W3,

681
00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:40.329
[S18] 50% chance.

682
00:41:41.110 --> 00:41:41.769
[S18] Okay?

683
00:41:41.769 --> 00:41:44.010
[S18] Well, if we look between W1 and W3,

684
00:41:44.010 --> 00:41:46.289
[S18] we're on the concave part of the utility function.

685
00:41:47.210 --> 00:41:50.010
[S18] And as a result, I will not take that gamble.

686
00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:51.809
[S18] That gamble leads me to point B,

687
00:41:53.050 --> 00:41:54.369
[S18] which is below B*.

688
00:41:54.809 --> 00:41:55.309
[S18] Okay?

689
00:41:55.309 --> 00:41:58.349
[S18] So basically, I will not take that gamble.

690
00:41:58.349 --> 00:42:00.110
[S18] That leaves me worse off than just having

691
00:42:00.110 --> 00:42:02.489
[S18] W1 plus W3 over 2 with certainty.

692
00:42:02.489 --> 00:42:04.349
[S18] This is just if you hold your hand...

693
00:42:04.349 --> 00:42:06.510
[S18] If you hold your hands with W1, W3,

694
00:42:06.510 --> 00:42:07.969
[S18] that's just the graph we saw before.

695
00:42:08.690 --> 00:42:09.690
[S18] You won't take that bet.

696
00:42:11.570 --> 00:42:14.090
[S18] But now, let's say, once you get above W3,

697
00:42:14.090 --> 00:42:16.769
[S18] once you're rich enough, you're risk-loving.

698
00:42:18.139 --> 00:42:20.489
[S18] So let's say people are risk averse at first,

699
00:42:20.489 --> 00:42:22.010
[S18] but then get risk-loving.

700
00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:23.510
[S18] So if you started at...

701
00:42:23.510 --> 00:42:27.889
[S18] If you said you were started at W3 plus W5 over 2,

702
00:42:27.889 --> 00:42:30.199
[S18] and if you would gamble between W3 and W5,

703
00:42:30.199 --> 00:42:33.199
[S18] then you're risk-loving in that range, and you take it.

704
00:42:34.449 --> 00:42:36.699
[S18] So once you get rich enough, you start to get risk-loving.

705
00:42:36.699 --> 00:42:38.110
[S18] So I talked about before getting richer,

706
00:42:38.110 --> 00:42:40.269
[S18] getting more and more risk-neutral as you get richer.

707
00:42:40.269 --> 00:42:41.110
[S18] What if it goes the other way?

708
00:42:41.110 --> 00:42:42.889
[S18] Well, you actually get risk-loving when you get richer.

709
00:42:43.730 --> 00:42:47.090
[S18] Well, then, if you think about the whole mega millions thing,

710
00:42:47.090 --> 00:42:49.389
[S18] you could see that over the whole distribution

711
00:42:49.389 --> 00:42:52.570
[S18] from W1 to W5, people might want to take that risk.

712
00:42:53.409 --> 00:42:55.650
[S18] That you could actually be risk-loving

713
00:42:55.650 --> 00:42:56.570
[S18] over an entire...

714
00:42:56.570 --> 00:42:59.210
[S18] Over these gambles, over these giant gambles.

715
00:42:59.210 --> 00:43:02.130
[S18] And that's just why people may play mega millions.

716
00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:04.389
[S18] That over the giant gambles, they're risk-loving,

717
00:43:04.389 --> 00:43:07.090
[S18] even if over more moderate gambles, they're risk averse.

718
00:43:07.849 --> 00:43:09.510
[S18] You don't insure yourself for a billion dollars.

719
00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:11.449
[S18] You insure yourself for a few thousand dollars.

720
00:43:11.449 --> 00:43:11.949
[S18] Yeah?

721
00:43:12.369 --> 00:43:13.610
[S19] How is the size of the gamble?

722
00:43:13.610 --> 00:43:15.289
[S19] We're talking about the term, and we're talking about like

723
00:43:15.289 --> 00:43:16.849
[S19] small bets that were more linear.

724
00:43:16.849 --> 00:43:19.510
[S19] Is it based on what the player puts in

725
00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:20.849
[S19] or what they get in return?

726
00:43:20.849 --> 00:43:21.769
[S18] Uh, both.

727
00:43:22.550 --> 00:43:23.110
[S18] Both.

728
00:43:23.110 --> 00:43:24.449
[S18] It's basically about...

729
00:43:24.449 --> 00:43:26.869
[S18] It's about expect utility calculations.

730
00:43:26.869 --> 00:43:28.170
[S18] Yes, it's true for mega millions,

731
00:43:28.170 --> 00:43:30.909
[S18] you put in $2, the chance of winning 1.6 billion.

732
00:43:30.909 --> 00:43:33.369
[S18] But your probability is way, way lower

733
00:43:33.369 --> 00:43:35.070
[S18] than 2 and 1.6 billion.

734
00:43:35.070 --> 00:43:36.090
[S18] So it's still unfair.

735
00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:37.849
[S18] Okay?

736
00:45:10.070 --> 00:45:12.250
[S20] Okay, my wife, against my better judgment,

737
00:45:12.250 --> 00:45:13.570
[S20] went out and bought a Mega Millions ticket.

738
00:45:14.570 --> 00:45:18.570
[S20] Okay, and she got utility out of waiting for that number to come up.

739
00:45:19.300 --> 00:45:21.400
[S20] And it was pretty cheap utility, it cost her two bucks.

740
00:45:22.429 --> 00:45:26.829
[S20] So in that sense, maybe you play the lottery a lot for entertainment.

741
00:45:27.469 --> 00:45:28.329
[S20] That's one theory.

742
00:45:29.150 --> 00:45:33.570
[S20] Unfortunately, the other theory is ignorance.

743
00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:39.000
[S20] That basically, you know, the saying is the lottery is a tax on the stupid.

744
00:45:39.969 --> 00:45:43.869
[S20] Okay, that basically just don't understand what a bad deal this is.

745
00:45:45.170 --> 00:45:48.369
[S20] And the problem is, we don't know which of these theories is right.

746
00:45:49.170 --> 00:45:52.469
[S20] And they have very different implications for government policy.

747
00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:53.670
[S20] Okay?

748
00:45:55.070 --> 00:45:58.070
[S20] If this theory is right, entertainment theory is right,

749
00:45:58.170 --> 00:46:00.070
[S20] then the government should support lotteries.

750
00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:04.469
[S20] Where essentially, the government is essentially getting paid for providing entertainment.

751
00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:08.429
[S20] It's what the lottery is often called a voluntary tax.

752
00:46:09.269 --> 00:46:11.829
[S20] That I am basically giving the government money that can run our schools

753
00:46:11.829 --> 00:46:13.599
[S20] and in return for the government giving me the entertainment value

754
00:46:13.599 --> 00:46:14.869
[S20] of seeing whether my scratch-off won.

755
00:46:15.170 --> 00:46:15.869
[S20] That's great.

756
00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:17.730
[S20] That's welfare improving.

757
00:46:19.369 --> 00:46:21.829
[S20] Under this theory, okay?

758
00:46:23.170 --> 00:46:26.500
[S20] We should be discouraging lotteries.

759
00:46:27.230 --> 00:46:29.030
[S20] That all we're doing is taking a bunch of ignorant people

760
00:46:29.030 --> 00:46:30.099
[S20] and wasting their money.

761
00:46:31.260 --> 00:46:31.760
[S20] Yeah?

762
00:46:33.130 --> 00:46:34.829
[S21] Is there not also the possibility that like,

763
00:46:35.300 --> 00:46:37.469
[S21] there is this concept of having nothing to lose?

764
00:46:37.469 --> 00:46:41.769
[S21] Like if you're already like too poor to be able to afford your basic needs,

765
00:46:42.130 --> 00:46:45.329
[S21] then you might be like, I may as well try and win the lottery

766
00:46:45.599 --> 00:46:47.000
[S21] and then I would be like all set.

767
00:46:47.269 --> 00:46:48.429
[S20] That is abso- that's-

768
00:46:49.369 --> 00:46:52.230
[S21] Not just be a tax on the stupid, but a tax on the already ready for it.

769
00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:53.400
[S20] That's exactly this.

770
00:46:54.400 --> 00:46:56.469
[S20] That wouldn't explain why I'd pay the $20 scratch-off.

771
00:46:57.199 --> 00:46:58.699
[S20] That's an absolute reason why I'd go ahead,

772
00:46:58.699 --> 00:47:00.570
[S20] even if I was starving and played the Mega Millions.

773
00:47:01.030 --> 00:47:03.269
[S20] And that's the Friedman-Savage hypothesis, absolutely.

774
00:47:03.469 --> 00:47:07.369
[S20] But that can't explain why, in fact, in some low-income communities,

775
00:47:07.630 --> 00:47:08.929
[S20] among some low-income groups,

776
00:47:09.070 --> 00:47:12.369
[S20] they'll spend as much as 20% of their income every year on the lottery.

777
00:47:12.869 --> 00:47:13.500
[S20] A net.

778
00:47:14.599 --> 00:47:15.099
[S20] Okay?

779
00:47:15.369 --> 00:47:18.130
[S20] They're losing huge amounts of money on scratch-off tickets.

780
00:47:19.599 --> 00:47:23.630
[S20] So the question is, is that a rational decision because they find it entertaining?

781
00:47:24.230 --> 00:47:27.730
[S20] Or an irrational decision because they just don't understand what's going on?

782
00:47:28.130 --> 00:47:30.599
[S20] And unfortunately, we know the answer,

783
00:47:30.599 --> 00:47:32.400
[S20] but we do know it's very important.

784
00:47:33.469 --> 00:47:36.869
[S20] It's important because it's big bucks and many, in many low-income communities,

785
00:47:37.030 --> 00:47:38.599
[S20] it's a huge source of expenditure.

786
00:47:39.469 --> 00:47:40.630
[S20] So I can't give you the answer today.

787
00:47:40.630 --> 00:47:41.800
[S20] I can just tell you it's an important question.

788
00:47:41.800 --> 00:47:44.329
[S20] I hope someday, you know, someone will figure out how to think about this

789
00:47:44.329 --> 00:47:45.730
[S20] because it's got very important implications.

790
00:47:46.300 --> 00:47:47.269
[S20] So let me stop there.

791
00:47:47.360 --> 00:47:48.670
[S20] That's what I want to say about uncertainty.

792
00:47:48.860 --> 00:47:51.929
[S20] And we'll come back and do another topic on Wednesday.
